.TH NETLIST 5
.SH NAME
netlist \- flat netlist of a circuit
.SH DESCRIPTION
A netlist lists the parts of a circuit and the nets between their
pins.
One record per line, words separated by blanks; # starts a comment.
.PP
.RS
.nf
part REF TYPE [KEY VALUE]...
net NAME [global] [class CLASS] REF.PIN...
pin REF.PIN nc|\-
.fi
.RE
.PP
A part record gives the designator and the type of a part (the symbol
or the pin table it is drawn by, as resistor or ti/lmh6609) and then
pairs of a key and a word:
.B value
(499, 0.1uF),
.B rating
(0.5W),
.B mpn
(the maker's part number),
.B swap
(the pins that may trade places, by number, as 1,2),
.B pins
(the names of the pins of a part with a pin table, as in+:3,out:6) and
.B name
(the name of the part in its source).
A net record names the net and lists its pins by designator and pin
number.
A global net is a rail or a port, the same net wherever its name is
used.
A net's class is a word of the source
.RI ( sch (5)),
power for a rail of a supply or ground flag; the board source maps it
to geometry
.RI ( pcb (5)).
Parts come before the nets that use them; a pin is on one net at
most.
A pin record, after the nets, is a pin on no net: nc if the source
leaves it unconnected on purpose,
.B \-
if not.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
part R401 resistor value 249R swap 1,2 name ch1.gain.rg
part U401 ti/lmh6609 value LMH6609 name ch1.gain.amp
net GND global class power R401.2
net ch1.gain.amp.in- R401.1 U401.2
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
schnet(1), netdiff(1), sch(5)
